"A second answer is that you are a living human animal. You are the same person as the child because you are the same organism, which has grown and replaced its matter as organisms do. This view, now called animalism, fits what biology tells us, and it explains why we identify one another by faces and fingerprints. Lockes objection to it has convinced most readers since. Should the soul of a prince, carrying with it the consciousness of the princes past life, enter and inform the body of a cobbler, everyone sees, he says, that the person who wakes in the cobblers bed is the prince. He remembers the palace and is accountable only for the princes actions. A modern version puts a brain in place of the soul. If your brain were transplanted into another body, most people judge that you would go where the brain goes. If so, we are not simply our bodies.": "9dc3bc501c44",
"A soul that makes no difference to anything we observe cannot be what we mean.": "9c0fa0f4fd45",
"Do the wishes she wrote down years ago still bind?": "0f3fd74dddae",
"Everything in this lesson would have been familiar to a Buddhist philosopher of two thousand years ago. The claim that a person is a body and a connected series of mental events, with no further owner, is the doctrine of anattā, not-self. The claim that the stages are tied together by causal continuity and not by a persisting thing is the Buddhist account of how one life, and one life after another, hangs together. Parfit knew this. Near the end of Reasons and Persons he observes that the Buddha would have agreed with him, and he prints passages from Buddhist texts in an appendix.": "c5605098b075",
"Find a photograph of yourself at the age of five. You have no doubt that the child is you. Yet almost nothing connects the two of you that you could point to. The child was a third of your height, knew a fraction of what you know, wanted different things, and was made of other matter. You remember perhaps a dozen moments from that whole year. If a stranger differed from you in all these ways, it would not occur to you to say that the two of you were one person. So what is the fact that makes it true of the child? The last unit asked this about a ship. Asking it about ourselves is harder, because we have a strong sense that in our own case there must be a definite answer.": "f9fece3d6397",
"For should the soul of a prince, carrying with it the consciousness of the princes past life, enter and inform the body of a cobbler, as soon as deserted by his own soul, every one sees he would be the same PERSON with the prince, accountable only for the princes actions: but who would say it was the same MAN?": "ce7f83477214",
"He is accountable only for the princes actions.": "e0cf1e70a208",
"In 1984 the Oxford philosopher Derek Parfit described a machine that puts the leading account under strain. You step into a booth on Earth. A scanner records the state of every cell in your body and, in doing so, destroys it. The information is sent to Mars, where a machine builds an exact replica out of local matter. The person who steps out on Mars has your memories, your character, and your plans, and believes that he has just made the journey. On the psychological theory this is you, and teleportation is a way of traveling.": "a53959ab7d46",
"It would not occur to you to say that the two of you were one person.": "e1cc46b8a7c9",
"Later philosophers repaired the theory. They replaced direct memory with overlapping chains of memory, and added the other connections that make up a mental life: intentions carried out, beliefs and character that persist. On the repaired view you are the child because a continuous series of such links runs from the child to you. This is the psychological continuity theory, and for most of the twentieth century it was the leading account.": "130a5942dd3c",
"Lockes own account was the first to locate identity in the minds contents. A person, he says, is a thinking intelligent being, that has reason and reflection, and can consider itself as itself, the same thinking thing, in different times and places. What makes a later person the same as an earlier one is consciousness reaching back: I am whoever I can remember being. The proposal was brilliant, and two objections were raised at once. Thomas Reid imagined a brave officer who remembers being flogged as a boy, and who later, as an old general, remembers his deeds as an officer and has forgotten the flogging. By Lockes test the general is the officer and the officer is the boy, and the general is not the boy, which breaks the logic of identity. Bishop Butler added that memory cannot make me the same person, because it assumes that I am. To remember doing something, as opposed to merely seeming to, I must be the one who did it.": "9cc66e1dfbb4",
"Now suppose the scanner is improved so that it no longer destroys the original. You step out of the booth on Earth, and a moment later you are speaking by video to someone on Mars who looks and thinks exactly as you do. Nobody believes that the two of you are one person. Yet the replica stands in exactly the same relation to you as before. Everything that made the first case a journey is still present. What is new is only that you have also survived in the ordinary way. If the replica is not you in the second case, it is hard to see how it was you in the first, and then the first case was a death. And if you decide that it was a death, you have to say why the copying of everything you value about yourself should count for nothing.": "229eee1b6e94",
"Parfit described a machine that puts the leading account under strain.": "2488aa9623d3",
"Parfits conclusion was radical. He argued that in these cases there is no further fact about whether the resulting person is me. We know everything there is to know once we know what happens to the body and how the mental states are connected. The question But will it be me? has no answer, and what is more surprising, it does not need one. What I have reason to care about is that there will be someone whose mental life continues mine, and that holds in the teleporter case whether or not we call it survival. Identity, he said, is not what matters. He reported that believing this changed his life. He had seemed to himself to be moving through a glass tunnel toward death, and afterward, he wrote, the walls of my glass tunnel disappeared. I now live in the open air.": "74e36f5f2163",
"The Western debate assumes that the self is something inside one person, and asks what it is. Classical Confucian thought starts somewhere else. A person is first of all a son or daughter, a parent, a friend, a subject or a minister, and to be good is to be these things well: let the ruler be a ruler, the minister a minister, the father a father, the son a son. On this view what I am cannot be stated without mentioning other people, and a human being with no relationships would be missing most of what makes a person. Zhuangzi goes further in the other direction. One of his characters comes out of a trance and says, Just now I lost myself (今者吾喪我). The Chinese uses two different words for I, and commentators have long taken the sentence to distinguish the self that we construct and defend from whatever it is that is left when that construction is dropped.": "cb8d91f4b7db",
"The concern attaches to something less sharp-edged than we suppose.": "bc70eac747cf",
"The first lesson of a new shelf in this series takes up the comparison in detail: which Western view comes closest to not-self, where the resemblance stops, and what the Buddhist teaching adds. It is the companion to this page.": "006473bc635d",
"The oldest answer is that you are a soul, an immaterial thing that stays the same while body and mind change around it. Plato held this, and Descartes gave it its modern form: I am a thinking substance. The view has the advantage of giving a definite answer in every case. Its weakness was pointed out by Locke. A soul, as the theory describes it, has no features by which it could be recognized. If the soul in your body were replaced each night by another exactly like it, carrying the same memories, neither you nor anyone else would notice. A soul that makes no difference to anything we can observe cannot be what we mean when we say that this is the same person we met last year.": "984db5dcba33",
"These puzzles are sometimes dismissed as science fiction. Their consequences are practical. When a person with advanced dementia no longer remembers her own life, families ask whether the wishes she wrote down years ago still bind, and the answer depends on whether she is the same person who wrote them. When a crime comes to trial forty years late, we ask whether the old man in the dock should be punished for what the young man did. Every promise assumes that the one who keeps it will be the one who made it. And each of us is concerned about one death in particular. If Parfit is right, that concern attaches to something less sharp-edged than we suppose. If he is wrong, there is some further thing that makes you you, and after two and a half thousand years of looking nobody has yet said convincingly what it is.": "d696e65223fa",
"To remember doing something, as opposed to merely seeming to.": "4aafe66cf606",
"Two objections were raised at once.": "444a5679f284",
"Why should the copying of everything you value count for nothing?": "4762322cc6e3",
"You step out of the booth on Earth, unharmed.": "be51f161ec48",
